{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: full N4 guidance design at ec5e99ef9874341205430dead8b5f29b70938f1b.\n\nOrdinary assigned binding\nOriginal design request 5fee04f47e18a42024bbf642572045255b89c0a5, builder promise bc53003980790ca4e65e51018051bae2194f4514, invitation 408f4c0ea808d272631f56ca4de7e98b37dcb0ee and my promise a8b06270415a98bf72213dcc7c7347eb7ef0eb76. Primary and sole artifact f11ad1f9b1925ae0f83916e090ad669dba9da7a5: notes/2026-10-04-act-guidance.md. This original commission owes a Git specification: normal assigned design review, not evidence-only, implementation closure or runtime adoption.\n\nI independently read every byte of all 584 lines, 34,706 bytes, and the complete invitation and original commissioning/conditions. Raw event head/path and sole builder-promise basis checked. Blob fa9441969e6b2233fc987a7a8dff8803522603c3; UTF-8 SHA-256 58ac817c84276c893652a699e2837385de926dad542cd1d91ba515a5bc76142a. Registered detached checkout at this exact head is clean. A parallel static auditor independently read the whole note and pinned source seams; I verified the findings against my own reads. Full review includes sections1–15, both planner-direction reconciliation tables, optional unadopted sketch, G1–G4 and the complete nine-row cold scenario.\n\nP2: signed settlement must include recorded refusals\nEvidence: section6 lines271–278 says saved bytes of an act already accepted return the original record, then says an act not accepted before is judged at admission now. That divides at acceptance, while the contract divides at recording. At d691e6e9, docs/protocol.md R-IDEM-2 lines524–527 expressly includes an earlier accepted act OR recorded refusal: identical canonical bytes return the original record OR refusal, create no entry and do so after key revocation. R-IDEM-4 lines533–534 assigns fresh admission only to an unrecorded refusal. Admission.ts294–314 and352–360 return the retained ActRecord or Refusal for exact bytes before current authority admission.\nTrigger and impact: a signed act is refused at a recorded step, its response is lost, then the policy or key authority changes before retry. It was not accepted, but the exact replay must recover its historical refusal. The draft's fresh-admission branch wrongly suggests it can be re-evaluated under current authority or become a new accepted action. Section3 already describes policy refusals as retained known outcomes; section6 must agree.\nCorrection: say accepted act or recorded refusal and original record or refusal. Use not recorded before for fresh admission, with unrecorded refusal distinguished. Preserve exact saved bytes/context, no private key needed merely to resend, and the separate bearer rule: an unusable bearer session cannot authenticate even its retry. A deliberate new intent after a known refusal remains different from settlement. Carry this into the shared explanation and G1's retained-outcome control, reusing existing idempotency evidence instead of adding platform guard sweeps. Confidence high; small design correction; risk low. This is a specification defect, not a new execution finding.\n\nP2: carry task-data authority into the existing emitted instructions\nEvidence: principle2.6 lines70–75 says room-authored label/help/reason/fix is attributed task data to weigh against the person's task and current authority. G2/G3 lines512–513 name tool result text and command/page output. Yet the same pinned source emits unconditional do-the-fix instructions: packages/mcp/src/tools.ts99 in the refusal output-schema description; tools.ts588 in MCP server INSTRUCTIONS; packages/client/src/agents-md.ts48 in the generated agent guide. The latter also gives unconditional rule-name recovery advice at49–51, including treating not-holder as lease expiry and telling the caller to claim again. Section4.1 explicitly explains why a rule name can instead be room policy and cannot justify that inference.\nImpact: an implementation limited to the named result formatter can provide careful qualified guidance while its initialization instructions, tool descriptions or generated AGENTS block still tell the agent to follow room-authored fixes or infer a recovery from the rule name. The cold run uses these discovery/instruction surfaces. Two contradictory recovery models would remain, with the imperative model encouraging unintended actions or loops.\nCorrection: name these existing instruction/description/generated-guide surfaces in the implementing handoff, assigning the client/MCP/N6 owners as appropriate. Align their prose with principles2.3,2.5,2.6 and section4.1: read and assess suggestions against the task, current authority, provenance and known outcome; do not automatically execute a fix or choose new intent. Qualify existing rule-name advice rather than letting it override evidence-based classification. Preserve public tool names, schema data shapes, authority checks and the shared explanation boundary. Description/instruction prose can be reconciled without introducing a new schema field or authoritative read. If N6 owns a portion, state its concrete coordination dependency so G4 cannot claim the coherent guide is delivered while conflicting text is still emitted. A focused room-authored fix/rule-collision control through the actual emitted guidance is appropriate; do not duplicate every refusal table row. Confidence high; small handoff correction; risk low.\n\nArchitecture conclusion\nThe shared transport-neutral public client explanation module, policy-owned declarations and MCP-owned eligibility predicate form a coherent boundary. One explanation table replaces multiple formatters. The note correctly puts reliable phase/receipt evidence before classification, preserves unknown writes and splits land's write from its later wait. The 43 platform refusal names are all present; I independently derived that count from the pinned PlatformRule declaration. Unknown names/source ambiguity cannot imply new authority or software-version claims. G1–G4, public export review and separately owned C3/C5/starter stores remain appropriate. The two corrections close inconsistencies without adding protocol/runtime authority.\n\nSecurity conclusion\nThe governing policy version matters for a recorded refusal; current policy and incomplete traces cannot prove its source. Platform and room policy rule IDs can collide because policy validation reserves only admin-approval at this pin. Unclassified fallback, unchanged Room result/words, hints based only on an authorized snapshot and catalogue, grantor role under delegation, exact signed binding and unknown-authority fallback are sound. Hints neither consult target/policy admission nor promise it. The signed-versus-bearer and lost-token distinctions are sound after the recorded-refusal correction. The emitted do-the-fix instructions must be brought into agreement with the task-data principle. No additional security defect was established by scoped static inspection; no provider, credential, revocation or agent behavior was probed.\n\nSimplification conclusion\nKeep text guidance, at most four short runtime lines after attributed Room words, existing tools and result shapes, shared ownership, no new caller read and the separately unadopted section13 sketch. Runtime choices must remain understandable without N6 links. Keep one bounded cold run, reuse stage5/client lost-response and revocation evidence only within its actual scope, and use focused boundary controls rather than per-rule tests or a second whole run. No first-Jam gate or vocabulary freeze is introduced.\n\nImplementation requirements already implied by the design\nCarry reliable phase and the accepted landing receipt/operation into the formatter before a later wait or lookup can fail. Pinned MCP run.ts211–221 throws on non-timeout wait errors and can fail its timeout fallback; callTool425–428 currently shapes the resulting error without that retained receipt. The later G2 implementation must supply its internal phase context, preserve a known accepted act through a failed read, and not infer phase from an absent maybeRecorded field. This is required by sections3 and5, not a separate newly claimed design defect.\n\nCaller snapshot retrieval, ancillary evidence reads and formatting must be best effort: their failure cannot replace a known Room outcome, block exact settlement, create new intent or expand a grant. With insufficient evidence, fall back to attributed authoritative result plus unknown authority/unclassified guidance. Preserve the existing MCP error/result distinction and structured data. Inventory checking should cover the ten ErrorCode values as well as all43 PlatformRule names, with generic uncertain/error fallbacks where classification lacks evidence; no ad hoc table-copying in each surface.\n\nComplete cold acceptance retained\nA fresh-context agent gets the short N6 guide and discovery, no custom hardcoded application tool or protocol/source spelunking. The unknown application is the exact desk specification or a qualified actual Jam. Discovery/absent summarize, deliberate exclusive-scope conflict, external review wait, operation follow-up, stale old binding followed by deliberate new binding and required regrant, forbidden accept, one lost reply settled without changing intent, and Pat's recorded acceptance all remain required. The run records turns/tokens/tool calls/help/refusals/failures, authoritative agreement, deliberate intent and the UI/agent's same operation/uncertainty where that journey is available. State finite run limits and qualify an unfinished/budget-limited run; do not reduce acceptance scope or treat a timeout as completion. One legitimate starter/Jam witness may serve both commissions where it actually satisfies them. Optional comparison remains qualified, no unsupported statistical claim.\n\nDependencies stay explicit\nStage5/MCP deployment, N6, the unfamiliar application and C5's separate journey remain separately owed. N3 correction2a0294fc identifies a nonholder proposal Git access and canonical log clone credential gap; required coding and independently controlled review in this cold run inherit that relevant dependency rather than receiving a hidden write credential. It also identifies starter-owned bearer/context witnesses; guidance does not itself deliver that durable store. This verdict neither reruns nor replaces the N3 review. Dated candidate inventory remains historical. Implementation is commissioned only after reviewed design/adoption, and every later lane needs ordinary independent exact-head source review and its authorized focused checks.\n\nMethod and limits\nFull frozen document and raw artifact/provenance/blob/SHA verification, normal assigned prepare, and selected static source reads at d691e6e9: contract errors, full MCP server/toolset source, tool declarations/run excerpts, client prepared/replay and generated guide, protocol idempotency and policy reserved IDs. No tests/build/typecheck/install, repository code execution, private diagnostic, provider effect, deployment, publication or external-person communication. No new whole-platform runtime audit. Proposed commands, public export, guidance and cold run remain unbuilt/unrun here. Evidence retained at /tmp/artroom-checker-n4-ec5e-c6qh2I.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f11ad1f9b1925ae0f83916e090ad669dba9da7a5","binding":"assigned","head":"ec5e99ef9874341205430dead8b5f29b70938f1b","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a8b06270415a98bf72213dcc7c7347eb7ef0eb76\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bc53003980790ca4e65e51018051bae2194f4514\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:62e51f0eee24459fba128582a067681511783f85","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}